<urn:soundlore:facet:RHY> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Rhythm, meter and tempo"@en ;
  skos:notation "RHY" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers the organization, perceived movement, and speed of musical time, including beat grouping, rhythmic relationships, and changes in pace."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Rhythm concerns when musical events happen and how their durations and accents relate. Meter provides a recurring framework for grouping beats, while tempo describes their rate. These dimensions interact, but they are distinct: the same rhythm can be performed at different speeds, and a steady tempo can support several patterns of accent. Listeners may perceive a pulse even when no instrument explicitly marks every beat.\n\nThe facet has four main branches. Meter includes regular beat groupings, changing meters, and overlapping metrical frameworks. Rhythmic feel describes how musicians shape the movement of a passage, including straight, swung, half-time, and laid-back approaches. Rhythmic devices covers relationships such as syncopation, polyrhythm, and rhythmic displacement. Tempo includes broad speed descriptions and ways of changing or stretching the pace.\n\nThese features help distinguish musical traditions without defining them alone. Waltzes commonly organize movement around three beats, much rock emphasizes a regular pulse with prominent backbeats, and some West African ensemble traditions combine contrasting rhythmic layers. This facet describes the temporal principles behind such differences. Specific drum grooves and percussion executions belong in Drums and percussion patterns; the organization of sections belongs in Form and structure. A genre can contain several meters, feels, or tempos."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000001, sl:SL000002, sl:SL000003, sl:SL000004, sl:SL000005, sl:SL000006, sl:SL000007, sl:SL000008, sl:SL000009, sl:SL000010, sl:SL000011, sl:SL000012, sl:SL000013, sl:SL000014, sl:SL000015, sl:SL000016, sl:SL000017, sl:SL000018, sl:SL000019, sl:SL000020, sl:SL000021, sl:SL000022, sl:SL000023, sl:SL000024, sl:SL000025, sl:SL000026, sl:SL000027, sl:SL000028, sl:SL000029, sl:SL000030, sl:SL000031, sl:SL000032, sl:SL000033, sl:SL000034, sl:SL000035, sl:SL000036, sl:SL000037, sl:SL000038, sl:SL000039, sl:SL000040, sl:SL000041, sl:SL000042, sl:SL000043, sl:SL000044, sl:SL000045, sl:SL000046, sl:SL000047, sl:SL000048, sl:SL000049, sl:SL000050, sl:SL000051, sl:SL000052, sl:SL000053, sl:SL000054, sl:SL000055, sl:SL000056, sl:SL000057, sl:SL000058, sl:SL000059, sl:SL000060, sl:SL000061, sl:SL000062 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:DRM> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Drums and percussion patterns"@en ;
  skos:notation "DRM" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers recurring drum and percussion grooves, along with playing techniques that shape their accents, textures, and rhythmic impact."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Drums and percussion give rhythm a particular physical and sonic form. A pattern distributes strikes, rests, and accents among instruments or playing surfaces. Its identity depends on more than the placement of beats: differences in touch, sound, timing, and interaction can make similar patterns feel substantially different. Percussion can establish a steady foundation, answer a melody, mark transitions, or become the main musical focus.\n\nThe facet is organized into Drum patterns and Drum techniques. Patterns include recognizable grooves such as four-on-the-floor, dembow, one-drop, and breakbeat-based approaches. Techniques describe how those patterns are performed or embellished, including ghost notes, rolls, flams, fills, brush playing, and linear drumming. A technique can appear within many patterns, while a pattern can be realized with acoustic instruments, electronic drums, or samples.\n\nPercussion is often a strong clue to genre. Reggae one-drop patterns organize emphasis differently from the continuous kick pulse common in much house music. Samba ensembles distribute rhythmic roles across several percussion instruments, creating a collective groove. These examples also show why an isolated pattern cannot identify every recording reliably. General concepts such as meter, syncopation, and tempo belong in Rhythm, meter and tempo. Instrument identities belong in Instrumentation, while processing and editing percussion sounds belong in Production and sound."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000063, sl:SL000064, sl:SL000065, sl:SL000066, sl:SL000067, sl:SL000068, sl:SL000069, sl:SL000070, sl:SL000071, sl:SL000072, sl:SL000073, sl:SL000074, sl:SL000075, sl:SL000076, sl:SL000077, sl:SL000078, sl:SL000079, sl:SL000080, sl:SL000081, sl:SL000082, sl:SL000083, sl:SL000084, sl:SL000085, sl:SL000086, sl:SL000087, sl:SL000088, sl:SL000089, sl:SL000090, sl:SL000091, sl:SL000092, sl:SL000093, sl:SL000094, sl:SL000095, sl:SL000096, sl:SL000097, sl:SL000098, sl:SL000099, sl:SL000100, sl:SL000101, sl:SL000102, sl:SL000103, sl:SL000104, sl:SL000105, sl:SL000106, sl:SL000107, sl:SL000108, sl:SL000109 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:HAR> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Harmony and tonality"@en ;
  skos:notation "HAR" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers simultaneous pitch relationships, tonal organization, chord movement, musical closure, and changes between tonal centers."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Harmony concerns how pitches sound together and how those combinations relate over time. Tonality describes an organization in which a particular pitch or chord acts as a center of reference. Music can establish that center strongly, move between centers, combine several, or avoid a clear tonal center. Harmonic tension and resolution depend on musical context and learned expectations; they are not universal measures of emotional meaning.\n\nThe facet has six main branches. Tonality describes broad systems of pitch organization. Chords identifies simultaneous pitch combinations. Harmonic devices covers resources such as pedal points, borrowed chords, and reharmonization. Chord progressions describes recurring sequences, while Cadences concerns gestures of arrival or closure. Modulation covers movement between tonal centers. These branches connect individual sonorities with the larger direction of a piece.\n\nHarmony helps distinguish styles through customary resources and their use. Western classical repertories often organize phrases around tonal movement and cadences. Jazz frequently develops extended chords and reharmonization, while blues makes distinctive use of recurring harmonic patterns. These are tendencies rather than requirements for every piece. Pitch collections considered as scales or modes belong in Scales and modes. The movement of a melodic line belongs in Melody and motif, and the interaction of simultaneous parts belongs in Texture and counterpoint."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000110, sl:SL000111, sl:SL000112, sl:SL000113, sl:SL000114, sl:SL000115, sl:SL000116, sl:SL000117, sl:SL000118, sl:SL000119, sl:SL000120, sl:SL000121, sl:SL000122, sl:SL000123, sl:SL000124, sl:SL000125, sl:SL000126, sl:SL000127, sl:SL000128, sl:SL000129, sl:SL000130, sl:SL000131, sl:SL000132, sl:SL000133, sl:SL000134, sl:SL000135, sl:SL000136, sl:SL000137, sl:SL000138, sl:SL000139, sl:SL000140, sl:SL000141, sl:SL000142, sl:SL000143, sl:SL000144, sl:SL000145, sl:SL000146, sl:SL000147, sl:SL000148, sl:SL000149, sl:SL000150, sl:SL000151, sl:SL000152, sl:SL000153, sl:SL000154, sl:SL000155, sl:SL000156, sl:SL000157, sl:SL000158, sl:SL000159, sl:SL000160, sl:SL000161, sl:SL000162, sl:SL000163, sl:SL000164, sl:SL000165, sl:SL000166, sl:SL000167, sl:SL000168, sl:SL000169, sl:SL000170, sl:SL000171, sl:SL000172, sl:SL000173, sl:SL000174 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:SCL> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Scales and modes"@en ;
  skos:notation "SCL" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers pitch collections and modal frameworks used to organize melodies, harmonies, and characteristic musical relationships."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Scales provide ways of arranging pitches, often presented in ascending or descending order. Modes describe pitch organization through relationships to a center and characteristic degrees or intervals. A collection of notes does not determine how music will sound by itself: emphasis, melodic movement, tuning, ornamentation, and harmonic context all affect its identity. Two pieces using similar pitch material can therefore have very different characters.\n\nThe facet is organized into Scales, Modes, and Modal and tuning systems. The first two branches include major and minor resources, pentatonic collections, symmetrical scales, named modes, and scales such as the Phrygian dominant that are shared by several traditions. The third holds frameworks that go beyond a list of pitches. Maqam and raga, for example, also involve hierarchies of degrees, characteristic phrases, ornamentation, and intonation, while the gamelan tunings pelog and slendro are properties of a particular ensemble's instruments.\n\nPitch frameworks help describe differences among traditions. Major and minor resources are widespread in Western tonal music; pentatonic collections occur in many musical cultures; Indian classical performance develops melodies within raga frameworks. None of these resources belongs exclusively to one genre. This facet describes the pitch material and its organizing framework. Chord function and tonal movement belong in Harmony and tonality, while the actual shaping, ornamentation, and development of melodic lines belong in Melody and motif."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000175, sl:SL000176, sl:SL000177, sl:SL000178, sl:SL000179, sl:SL000180, sl:SL000181, sl:SL000182, sl:SL000183, sl:SL000184, sl:SL000185, sl:SL000186, sl:SL000187, sl:SL000188, sl:SL000196, sl:SL000197, sl:SL000198, sl:SL000199, sl:SL000189, sl:SL000190, sl:SL000191, sl:SL000192, sl:SL000193, sl:SL000194, sl:SL000195, sl:SL000200, sl:SL000201, sl:SL000202, sl:SL000822, sl:SL000823 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:MEL> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Melody and motif"@en ;
  skos:notation "MEL" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers recognizable musical lines, recurring melodic ideas, ways of transforming or decorating them, and practices of improvisation."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "A melody is a sequence of pitches and durations heard as a connected line. It may be sung, played by an instrument, or distributed among several performers. A motif is a shorter idea that can recur or develop within a piece. Melodic identity depends on rhythm and phrasing as well as pitch, so changing an idea’s timing can alter its character even when its notes remain similar.\n\nThe facet has three main branches. Melodic elements includes hooks, riffs, motifs, ostinatos, countermelodies, and other recognizable materials. Melodic devices describes ways of shaping a line, including sequences, chromatic movement, ornaments, and different relationships between successive notes. Improvisation concerns the creation or development of musical material during performance, including practices that respond to an established harmonic or modal framework.\n\nMelodic practices help distinguish traditions and styles. A rock riff can supply a recurring instrumental identity, a jazz solo can develop ideas through improvisation, and Indian classical performance can explore characteristic melodic movements within a raga. The significance of each example lies in how material is used, rather than in the presence of melody alone. Scales and modal frameworks belong in Scales and modes. Vocal production belongs in Voice and vocal technique, while the placement of melodic ideas across a whole piece belongs in Form and structure."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000203, sl:SL000204, sl:SL000205, sl:SL000206, sl:SL000207, sl:SL000208, sl:SL000824, sl:SL000209, sl:SL000210, sl:SL000211, sl:SL000212, sl:SL000213, sl:SL000214, sl:SL000215, sl:SL000216, sl:SL000217, sl:SL000218, sl:SL000219, sl:SL000825, sl:SL000826, sl:SL000220, sl:SL000221, sl:SL000827, sl:SL000222, sl:SL000223, sl:SL000224, sl:SL000225, sl:SL000226, sl:SL000227, sl:SL000228, sl:SL000229 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:FRM> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Form and structure"@en ;
  skos:notation "FRM" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers the arrangement of musical sections, patterns of repetition and contrast, and established frameworks for complete compositions."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Form describes how a piece unfolds over time. Listeners recognize beginnings, returns, contrasts, transitions, and endings through changes in melody, harmony, rhythm, texture, or sound. A structure may follow a familiar pattern or emerge through gradual development. Understanding form helps explain why a passage feels like preparation, arrival, interruption, or return, even when the listener cannot name the sections involved.\n\nThe facet is organized into Song sections, Song forms, and Compositional forms. Song sections identify parts such as verses, choruses, bridges, introductions, breakdowns, and outros. Song forms describe how such parts are arranged and repeated, including verse–chorus and strophic designs. Compositional forms cover broader established frameworks and types of composition, including fugues, rondos, suites, concertos, and other resources used across different repertories.\n\nFormal habits can provide clues to genre. Many popular songs alternate verses and choruses; some electronic dance arrangements organize anticipation around build-ups and drops; Western classical works may use recurring themes within a rondo or a larger sonata framework. Individual works can adapt or combine these practices. This facet concerns the placement and relationship of musical material across time. The material itself belongs in the relevant melodic, harmonic, rhythmic, or textural facet. Recording effects that create a transition belong in Production and sound, even when their structural role is described here."@en ;
