<urn:soundlore:facet:TEX> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Texture and counterpoint"@en ;
  skos:notation "TEX" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers how simultaneous musical parts combine, interact, and distribute melodic or accompanying roles."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Texture describes the overall relationship among musical parts sounding at the same time. Music may present one line, several closely coordinated parts, independent lines, or multiple versions of a shared melody. Texture also concerns density and the distribution of attention: a few widely spaced sounds can feel different from many overlapping parts. The number of instruments alone does not determine the texture.\n\nThe facet has three main branches. Texture includes broad configurations such as monophony, homophony, polyphony, and heterophony, alongside descriptions of density. Counterpoint concerns the interaction of distinct lines, including imitation and voice leading. Accompaniment textures describes ways of supporting or responding to a principal part, such as comping, drones, repeated figures, call and response, and antiphonal exchange.\n\nTextural habits can help distinguish musical traditions. Western choral hymns may coordinate voices in chordal movement. Jazz accompaniment can leave space around an improvised lead while contributing its own rhythmic and harmonic activity. Some Indonesian gamelan repertories combine interlocking and layered parts. Such relationships may change within a single piece. This facet describes what the parts do together. Their instrument identities belong in Instrumentation, their pitch combinations in Harmony and tonality, and their individual melodic ideas in Melody and motif. Studio layering belongs here when describing the resulting musical relationship, and in Production and sound when describing the recording process."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000318, sl:SL000319, sl:SL000320, sl:SL000321, sl:SL000322, sl:SL000323, sl:SL000324, sl:SL000325, sl:SL000326, sl:SL000327, sl:SL000328, sl:SL000329, sl:SL000330, sl:SL000331, sl:SL000332, sl:SL000333, sl:SL000334, sl:SL000335, sl:SL000336, sl:SL000337 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:VOC> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Voice and vocal technique"@en ;
  skos:notation "VOC" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers singing and spoken vocal sounds, voice categories, expressive delivery, and the organization of vocal parts."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The human voice can carry melody, rhythm, language, and expressive character at the same time. Vocal sound depends on how a performer coordinates production, resonance, articulation, and phrasing. Descriptions such as breathy, clean, shouted, or melismatic identify different aspects of delivery rather than fixed categories of singer. A performer may use several approaches within one song, and vocal terminology varies among musical traditions.\n\nThe facet is organized into five main branches. Voice types describes categories based on range, timbre, or performance role. Singing techniques covers approaches such as falsetto, belting, vibrato, and overtone singing. Harsh vocals groups deliberately rough or distorted vocal timbres. Spoken and rapped vocals concerns speech-centered and rhythmically organized delivery. Vocal arrangement describes the relationship among lead voices, backing parts, harmonies, and groups.\n\nVocal practices are often useful clues to style. Opera commonly emphasizes sustained, projected singing; many R&B performances use melismatic ornamentation; rap foregrounds the rhythmic placement and articulation of words. These practices also travel across genre boundaries. This facet describes vocal delivery and the organization of voices, rather than the literary content of the words, which belongs in Lyrics and prosody. Electronic pitch correction, vocoding, and recorded vocal distortion belong in Production and sound when the focus is the processing method. Melodic shapes shared with instrumental music belong in Melody and motif."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000338, sl:SL000339, sl:SL000340, sl:SL000341, sl:SL000342, sl:SL000343, sl:SL000344, sl:SL000345, sl:SL000346, sl:SL000347, sl:SL000348, sl:SL000349, sl:SL000350, sl:SL000351, sl:SL000352, sl:SL000353, sl:SL000354, sl:SL000355, sl:SL000356, sl:SL000357, sl:SL000358, sl:SL000359, sl:SL000360, sl:SL000361, sl:SL000362, sl:SL000363, sl:SL000364, sl:SL000365, sl:SL000366, sl:SL000367, sl:SL000368, sl:SL000369, sl:SL000370, sl:SL000371, sl:SL000372, sl:SL000373, sl:SL000374, sl:SL000375, sl:SL000376, sl:SL000377, sl:SL000378, sl:SL000379, sl:SL000380, sl:SL000381, sl:SL000382, sl:SL000383, sl:SL000384, sl:SL000385, sl:SL000386, sl:SL000387, sl:SL000388, sl:SL000389, sl:SL000390, sl:SL000391, sl:SL000392, sl:SL000393, sl:SL000394, sl:SL000395, sl:SL000396, sl:SL000397, sl:SL000398, sl:SL000399, sl:SL000400, sl:SL000401, sl:SL000402, sl:SL000403, sl:SL000404, sl:SL000405, sl:SL000406, sl:SL000407 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:INS> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Instrumentation"@en ;
  skos:notation "INS" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers the instruments, sound-producing resources, and ensembles used to perform or construct music."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Instrumentation describes which sound-producing resources take part in music. These may include acoustic instruments, electronic devices, percussion, or established ensembles. Instruments contribute characteristic ranges, attacks, resonances, and possibilities of expression, but their identities do not prescribe a single musical role. A guitar can provide melody, chords, bass movement, or percussion-like gestures, and electronic instruments can imitate familiar sounds or produce unfamiliar ones.\n\nThe facet is organized into Plucked strings, Bowed strings, Woodwinds, Brass, Keyboards, Electronic instruments, Percussion, and Ensembles. These branches provide practical routes through instrument families and performance groupings. They are browsing categories rather than one universal scientific classification: keyboard instruments, for example, produce sound through several different mechanisms. Electronic resources also include recognizable devices and sounds associated with particular production practices.\n\nInstrument combinations can strongly suggest a tradition. A string quartet identifies a familiar Western chamber ensemble; mariachi often combines strings with trumpets; Indonesian gamelan ensembles prominently use tuned percussion. Similar instruments may nevertheless appear in very different genres, and substitutions can preserve a musical role while changing its sound. This facet identifies the resources involved. How performers play them belongs in Instrumental techniques, the relationship among their parts in Texture and counterpoint, and recording or processing choices in Production and sound. Vocal delivery is developed separately in Voice and vocal technique."@en ;
