<urn:soundlore:facet:TEC> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Instrumental techniques"@en ;
  skos:notation "TEC" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers ways of playing and manipulating instruments that shape articulation, pitch, rhythm, timbre, and musical expression."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Instrumental technique describes the actions through which a performer produces and shapes sound. Different gestures can change a note’s attack, duration, pitch, resonance, or rhythmic emphasis. Techniques may be associated with a particular instrument, but related musical effects can arise through different physical methods. A connected phrase, for example, requires different actions on a guitar, a bowed string instrument, and a wind instrument.\n\nThe facet is organized into Guitar techniques, Bass techniques, Keyboard techniques, Bowed-string techniques, Wind techniques, and DJ techniques. These branches include picking and muting approaches, bass articulation, keyboard accompaniment patterns, bowing and plucking gestures, breath-related effects, and the manipulation of recorded material through turntables. Some entries describe recurring performance approaches, while others identify a particular gesture or sound-producing method.\n\nTechniques help give genres their recognizable detail. Flamenco guitar makes distinctive use of strumming and plucking approaches such as rasgueado and picado. Jazz bass often supplies motion through walking lines. Hip-hop turntablism uses scratching and related manipulation as musical performance. These resources can be adopted outside their customary settings. This facet focuses on performance methods. Instrument identities belong in Instrumentation, broadly applicable articulations belong in Dynamics and articulation, and audio processing such as compression, filtering, or reverberation belongs in Production and sound. A processed sound may combine contributions from both playing technique and studio treatment."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000584, sl:SL000585, sl:SL000586, sl:SL000587, sl:SL000588, sl:SL000589, sl:SL000590, sl:SL000591, sl:SL000592, sl:SL000863, sl:SL000864, sl:SL000593, sl:SL000594, sl:SL000595, sl:SL000596, sl:SL000597, sl:SL000598, sl:SL000599, sl:SL000600, sl:SL000601, sl:SL000602, sl:SL000603, sl:SL000604, sl:SL000605, sl:SL000606, sl:SL000607, sl:SL000608, sl:SL000609, sl:SL000610, sl:SL000611, sl:SL000612, sl:SL000613, sl:SL000614, sl:SL000615, sl:SL000616, sl:SL000617, sl:SL000619, sl:SL000620, sl:SL000621, sl:SL000622, sl:SL000623, sl:SL000624, sl:SL000625, sl:SL000627, sl:SL000628, sl:SL000629, sl:SL000630, sl:SL000865, sl:SL000631, sl:SL000632, sl:SL000633, sl:SL000634, sl:SL000635, sl:SL000636, sl:SL000637, sl:SL000638, sl:SL000639, sl:SL000640, sl:SL000641, sl:SL000642, sl:SL000643 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:PRD> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Production and sound"@en ;
  skos:notation "PRD" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers recording choices, audio processing, sampling, sound design, and production aesthetics that shape the audible character of music."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Production shapes how performed or constructed sounds become a recording or amplified presentation. Decisions about capture, editing, processing, balance, and space can change the listener’s experience substantially. Production may preserve the impression of an immediate performance or create a sound that could not arise from an unprocessed ensemble. Its contribution is audible even when listeners cannot identify the equipment or methods involved.\n\nThe facet organizes these choices through several branches. Recording aesthetic and Era aesthetic describe broad sonic approaches and historical references. Dynamics processing concerns signal-level control, while Spatial effects creates or modifies perceived space. Modulation effects, Distortion, Filters, and Pitch effects alter different properties of sound. Time manipulation changes timing, duration, or playback direction. Sampling reuses recorded material, and Sound design develops sounds for particular musical roles.\n\nProduction practices can help distinguish styles. Dub frequently gives echo and reverberation an active musical role. Some punk recordings emphasize a direct, relatively rough presentation. Contemporary pop often uses detailed editing and layered vocal production. These are tendencies, and recordings within each tradition can differ widely.\n\nThis facet concerns how sound is captured, transformed, and presented. The performer’s physical actions belong in Instrumental techniques or Voice and vocal technique. Musical loudness and articulation as expressive performance choices belong in Dynamics and articulation. Historical sound labels describe selected production traits rather than everything recorded during a particular decade."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000644, sl:SL000645, sl:SL000646, sl:SL000647, sl:SL000648, sl:SL000649, sl:SL000650, sl:SL000651, sl:SL000652, sl:SL000653, sl:SL000654, sl:SL000618, sl:SL000655, sl:SL000656, sl:SL000657, sl:SL000658, sl:SL000659, sl:SL000660, sl:SL000661, sl:SL000662, sl:SL000663, sl:SL000664, sl:SL000665, sl:SL000666, sl:SL000667, sl:SL000668, sl:SL000669, sl:SL000670, sl:SL000671, sl:SL000672, sl:SL000673, sl:SL000674, sl:SL000675, sl:SL000676, sl:SL000677, sl:SL000626, sl:SL000678, sl:SL000679, sl:SL000866, sl:SL000680, sl:SL000681, sl:SL000682, sl:SL000683, sl:SL000684, sl:SL000685, sl:SL000686, sl:SL000687, sl:SL000688, sl:SL000689, sl:SL000690, sl:SL000691, sl:SL000692, sl:SL000693, sl:SL000694, sl:SL000695, sl:SL000696, sl:SL000697, sl:SL000698, sl:SL000699, sl:SL000700, sl:SL000701, sl:SL000702, sl:SL000703, sl:SL000704, sl:SL000705, sl:SL000706, sl:SL000707, sl:SL000708, sl:SL000709, sl:SL000710, sl:SL000711, sl:SL000712, sl:SL000713, sl:SL000714, sl:SL000715, sl:SL000716, sl:SL000717, sl:SL000718 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:DYN> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Dynamics and articulation"@en ;
  skos:notation "DYN" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers expressive changes in musical intensity and the ways notes are attacked, sustained, connected, or separated."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Dynamics concerns the relative strength or intensity of musical events. Articulation concerns how notes begin, continue, and end, including their connection or separation. Together, they shape phrasing and emphasis: the same sequence of pitches can sound restrained, forceful, flowing, or sharply marked depending on its execution. Their effects depend on musical context, instrument, and the contrasts established within a passage.\n\nThe facet has two main branches. Dynamics includes quiet and loud performance, gradual increases and decreases, sudden emphasis, and contrasts between passages. Articulation includes approaches such as staccato, legato, marcato, and tenuto. These terms describe relationships within the music rather than fixed acoustic measurements. A quiet passage can remain intense, while a strong attack need not produce a long or sustained sound.\n\nExpressive handling helps distinguish performances and styles. Western classical repertories often use carefully shaped dynamic contrasts and articulation. Jazz phrasing can depend on subtle accents and differences in note length. Rock arrangements may create dramatic contrast between restrained verses and forceful choruses. No single pattern defines all examples of a genre. This facet describes musical expression through intensity and note shaping. Compression and limiting belong in Production and sound as signal-processing methods. Instrument-specific ways of achieving an articulation belong in Instrumental techniques, and emotional interpretations of the result belong in Mood and character."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000719, sl:SL000720, sl:SL000721, sl:SL000722, sl:SL000723, sl:SL000724, sl:SL000725, sl:SL000726, sl:SL000727, sl:SL000728, sl:SL000729, sl:SL000730, sl:SL000731 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:LYR> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Lyrics and prosody"@en ;
  skos:notation "LYR" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers the verbal construction of songs, including rhyme, sound patterns, narrative devices, and the relationship between words and musical delivery."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Lyrics contribute language, imagery, narrative, and verbal sound to music. Prosody concerns how features such as syllables, stress, rhythm, and phrasing interact with musical delivery. Words can align closely with musical accents or create expressive tension against them. Their meaning also changes through repetition, placement, and performance, so a lyric’s musical effect cannot be understood from its written text alone.\n\nThe facet is organized around Lyrical devices. This branch includes rhyme schemes, internal and multisyllabic rhyme, assonance, repetition, storytelling, wordplay, vocables, and other ways of shaping verbal material. It also includes broad approaches to lyrical content, such as protest, confession, and abstraction. These entries describe resources that can coexist: a narrative song can use repeated phrases, intricate rhyme, and ambiguous imagery at the same time.\n\nLyrical practices help distinguish traditions and individual styles. Rap often gives particular prominence to rhyme placement and verbal rhythm. Folk ballads frequently organize words around a developing story. Some flamenco singing stretches or repeats words for expressive emphasis. These practices are shared and adapted across genres. This facet concerns the words and their organization in music. Vocal timbre and delivery belong in Voice and vocal technique, while melodic ornamentation belongs in Melody and motif. The facet also records the absence of words: Instrumental marks pieces without sung or spoken lyrics."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000732, sl:SL000733, sl:SL000734, sl:SL000735, sl:SL000736, sl:SL000737, sl:SL000738, sl:SL000739, sl:SL000740, sl:SL000741, sl:SL000742, sl:SL000743, sl:SL000744, sl:SL000745, sl:SL000746, sl:SL000747, sl:SL000748 .

<urn:soundlore:facet:MOO> a skos:Collection ;
  skos:prefLabel "Mood and character"@en ;
  skos:notation "MOO" ;
  skos:definition "This facet covers the perceived emotional and expressive qualities of music, including atmosphere, energy, intimacy, tension, and dramatic character."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Mood and character describe how music is experienced rather than a single measurable feature of its construction. Listeners may hear a passage as playful, ominous, contemplative, triumphant, or intimate because of interacting musical cues and personal associations. Rhythm, timbre, register, harmony, dynamics, and words can all contribute. Interpretations also depend on cultural familiarity, listening circumstances, and what has happened earlier in the piece.\n\nThe facet is organized under Mood. Its concepts provide a vocabulary for broad expressive impressions, including uplifting, melancholic, aggressive, dreamy, mysterious, and spiritual qualities. Several descriptions can apply simultaneously or to different moments of one recording. A piece may combine energetic movement with dark timbres, or a restrained performance with strong emotional intensity. These terms therefore support description without imposing a single emotional identity.\n\nCharacter can help compare genres, but its value depends on context. Listeners may hear playful energy in funk, solemnity in a devotional chant, or grandeur in an orchestral passage. None of these impressions belongs exclusively to that tradition. This facet records the perceived expressive result. The rhythmic, harmonic, instrumental, and production features contributing to it belong in their respective facets. Bright timbre and a bright mood, for example, are related descriptions with different meanings. Mood labels should not be treated as automatic consequences of major or minor tonality, tempo, or instrumentation."@en ;
  skos:member sl:SL000749, sl:SL000750, sl:SL000751, sl:SL000752, sl:SL000753, sl:SL000754, sl:SL000755, sl:SL000756, sl:SL000757, sl:SL000758, sl:SL000759, sl:SL000760, sl:SL000761, sl:SL000762, sl:SL000763, sl:SL000764, sl:SL000765, sl:SL000766, sl:SL000767, sl:SL000768, sl:SL000769, sl:SL000770, sl:SL000771, sl:SL000772 .

sl:SL000001 a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:prefLabel "Meter"@en ;
  skos:notation "SL000001"^^slv:UniqueID ;
  skos:notation "RHY.01"^^slv:TreeNumber ;
  skos:altLabel "time signature"@en ;
  skos:definition "The recurring organization of musical beats into groups, usually represented by a time signature and patterns of strong and weak beats."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Organization of beats into bars with recurring accents."@en ;
  skos:topConceptOf <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:narrower sl:SL000002, sl:SL000006, sl:SL000010, sl:SL000018, sl:SL000019, sl:SL000020, sl:SL000021 ;
  skos:exactMatch wd:Q630649 ;
  rdfs:seeAlso <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metre_(music)> ;
  slv:promptTerm "time signature" ;
  slv:promptAdherenceExpected "low" ;
  slv:definitionStatus "draft" .

sl:SL000002 a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:prefLabel "Simple meter"@en ;
  skos:notation "SL000002"^^slv:UniqueID ;
  skos:notation "RHY.01.01"^^slv:TreeNumber ;
  skos:definition "A meter in which each main beat normally divides into two equal parts, as in 2/4, 3/4, or 4/4."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Each beat divides into two."@en ;
  skos:broader sl:SL000001 ;
  skos:narrower sl:SL000003, sl:SL000004, sl:SL000005 ;
  slv:promptAdherenceExpected "low" ;
  slv:definitionStatus "draft" .

sl:SL000003 a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:prefLabel "2/4 time"@en ;
  skos:notation "SL000003"^^slv:UniqueID ;
  skos:notation "RHY.01.01.01"^^slv:TreeNumber ;
  skos:definition "A time signature with two quarter-note beats per bar, often heard in marches, polkas, and other duple-meter music."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Two beats per bar; marches, polka, cumbia."@en ;
  skos:broader sl:SL000002 ;
  skos:related sl:SL000289 ;
  slv:promptTerm "2/4 time" ;
  slv:promptAdherenceExpected "low" ;
  slv:definitionStatus "draft" .

sl:SL000004 a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:prefLabel "3/4 time"@en ;
  skos:notation "SL000004"^^slv:UniqueID ;
  skos:notation "RHY.01.01.02"^^slv:TreeNumber ;
  skos:definition "A time signature with three quarter-note beats per bar, commonly associated with waltzes and other triple-meter music."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Three beats per bar; waltz, mazurka, many folk ballads."@en ;
  skos:broader sl:SL000002 ;
  skos:related sl:SL000290 ;
  slv:promptTerm "3/4 time", "waltz time" ;
  slv:promptAdherenceExpected "medium" ;
  slv:definitionStatus "draft" .

sl:SL000005 a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:prefLabel "4/4 time"@en ;
  skos:notation "SL000005"^^slv:UniqueID ;
  skos:notation "RHY.01.01.03"^^slv:TreeNumber ;
  skos:altLabel "common time"@en ;
  skos:definition "A time signature with four quarter-note beats per bar, widely used in popular music and also called common time."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "The default meter of nearly all popular music."@en ;
  skos:broader sl:SL000002 ;
  slv:promptTerm "4/4" ;
  slv:promptAdherenceExpected "low" ;
  slv:definitionStatus "draft" .

sl:SL000006 a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:prefLabel "Compound meter"@en ;
  skos:notation "SL000006"^^slv:UniqueID ;
  skos:notation "RHY.01.02"^^slv:TreeNumber ;
  skos:definition "A meter in which each main beat normally divides into three equal parts, as in 6/8, 9/8, or 12/8."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Each beat divides into three."@en ;
  skos:broader sl:SL000001 ;
  skos:narrower sl:SL000007, sl:SL000008, sl:SL000009 ;
  slv:promptAdherenceExpected "low" ;
  slv:definitionStatus "draft" .

sl:SL000007 a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <urn:soundlore:scheme> ;
  skos:prefLabel "6/8 time"@en ;
  skos:notation "SL000007"^^slv:UniqueID ;
  skos:notation "RHY.01.02.01"^^slv:TreeNumber ;
  skos:definition "A time signature usually felt as two dotted-quarter-note beats per bar, each divided into three eighth notes."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote "Two triple-divided beats; jig, Irish ballads, slow doo-wop."@en ;
  skos:broader sl:SL000006 ;
  skos:related sl:SL000026, sl:SL000020 ;
  slv:promptTerm "6/8 time" ;
  slv:promptAdherenceExpected "medium" ;
